## HOW TO REVISE
- Address every issue listed above. Work the high-severity ones first.
- Keep everything the grader did not object to. This is a revision, not a
  re-extraction from scratch — silently dropping correct facts is itself a
  regression.
- **Coverage findings are instructions to grow the graph.** When the grader says
  something is missing, the fix is new entities and new relations, not a rewording
  of what is already there. A revision that returns the same number of facts has
  not addressed a MISSING_ENTITY or MISSING_RELATION finding.
- **Adding an entity means adding its edges.** An entity the grader asked for,
  added with nothing connected to it, is not a fix — it is a new isolated node and
  the summary still cannot say anything about it. Go back to the documents for the
  quotes that link it.
- Removing a hallucinated entity means also removing every relation that
  referenced it, or the graph will fail referential integrity.
- If you believe an issue is mistaken, keep the original fact and make sure its
  evidence quote is verbatim and unambiguous — that is what a disagreement
  looks like here.
- Retyping an edge changes the sentence the summarizer will write from it, so
  apply a suggested label only where the quote genuinely supports it. Trading
  one vague label for a specific one that overstates the evidence is not a fix.

The evidence rule is unchanged, including for everything you add: exact quotes,
one or two per relation, from the same document.

Re-emit the COMPLETE corrected graph, not a diff.

